- [Blog](https://boat.dev/blog): benchmarks and infrastructure notes
- [boat vs E2B: full VMs for long runs vs fast-resume microVMs](https://boat.dev/compare/boat-vs-e2b): boat vs E2B: boat runs full VMs from $0.036/h with nested virtualization and no session cap once auto-stop is off. E2B pauses with memory and wakes on request.
- [boat vs Daytona: a full VM for every sandbox](https://boat.dev/compare/boat-vs-daytona): boat gives every sandbox its own VM. Daytona starts with containers and adds Linux VM, Windows, macOS and GPU classes. Limits and prices compared.
- [boat vs Modal: persistent VMs vs serverless sandboxes](https://boat.dev/compare/boat-vs-modal): boat vs Modal Sandboxes: full VMs that save their disk on their own and resume after a stop, versus gVisor or VM sandboxes with GPUs and global regions.
- [boat vs Vercel Sandbox: all-day agent VMs with a desktop](https://boat.dev/compare/boat-vs-vercel-sandbox): boat vs Vercel Sandbox compared: session length, saved files, desktop and browser, agent APIs, CPU speed, regions, egress rules and the cost of real workloads.
- [boat vs Blaxel: full Linux VMs vs instant-resume microVMs](https://boat.dev/compare/boat-vs-blaxel): boat gives each agent a full Linux VM with KVM, systemd, SSH and a disk. Blaxel runs Firecracker microVMs that sleep after the last connection and wake in 25 ms.
- [boat vs Runloop: CPU speed, full VMs and network controls](https://boat.dev/compare/boat-vs-runloop): boat and Runloop both run AI agents in VMs. Compare CPU results, nested virtualization, saved files, network rules, own-cloud deploys, start limits and costs.
- [boat vs Tensorlake: full VMs vs fast-snapshot microVMs](https://boat.dev/compare/boat-vs-tensorlake): Compare boat and Tensorlake Sandboxes: full VMs on boat's own hosts with built-in coding agents versus Firecracker microVMs with memory snapshots and BYOC.
